prompt:
Build ONE self-contained HTML file: a 2D physics earthquake-resistance simulator rendered on <canvas data-testid="simulation">. A tower of at least 6 stories made of beams and joints (cross-braced) stands on ground that shakes horizontally with adjustable intensity (0-10) and frequency (0.1-5 Hz). Requirements: beams are colored by stress (green -> yellow -> red); a beam BREAKS when its stress exceeds a material limit, and broken pieces fall under gravity and collide with the ground (nothing may tunnel out of the world or fall through the floor). UI: sliders or number inputs with data-testid="intensity" and data-testid="frequency" plus live readouts, and <button data-testid="reset">Reset</button>.

MANDATORY benchmark API — a hidden automated grader calls exactly this; it must exist on window before load finishes and be independent of wall-clock time:
window.BENCH = {
  reset(seed) {},              // rebuild the identical tower (same geometry/materials) and seed all RNG
  step(ms) {},                 // advance physics by exactly ms milliseconds of simulated time (fixed-dt substeps), ground shaking included
  perform(action, payload) {}, // 'setIntensity' {v: 0..10}, 'setFrequency' {v: hz}
  getState() { return {seed, timeMs, standing, collapsed, brokenBeams}; },
  getMetrics() { return {intensity, frequency, maxStress, toppledDeg}; }
};
standing (boolean): >= 80% of joints remain within 20% below their original heights. collapsed (boolean): top of tower below 40% of original height, OR more than 50% of beams broken. toppledDeg: maximum tilt (degrees) of the top section from vertical.
The page must also animate on its own via requestAnimationFrame when the grader is not driving it. No external libraries — implement the beam/joint physics yourself in vanilla JS on canvas 2D.
